00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:14.370
Pat me on the back and 
dirt flies off in every direction!

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:20.420
Plenty of companies have begun treating

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:24.520
PeopleLog profiles as though 
they're official identification.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:28.010
Should we start using it for passports?

00:17:28.370 --> 00:17:33.370
It's a much more cost-effective way of 
preventing crime than the police, too.

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:39.520
I've just returned from 
the Kingdom of Padar.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.470
Padar?

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:45.530
They've been testing the effectiveness 
of PeopleLog as a tool of government.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:51.380
In regards to taxation, law enforcement, 
and public management in Padar,

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:55.700
PeopleLog is being adopted to deal with 
the majority of administrative tasks.

00:17:56.300 --> 00:18:00.490
A distributed network, public or private, 
with such a wealth of information

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:03.070
lessens the need for centralized 
control by the government.

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:07.370
With the voluntary submission of 
personal data via social media,

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:09.930
all public services have seen 
a significant increase in efficiency,

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.520
including the police.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:12.560
That's insane...

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:16.000
Efficiency aside, we must consider 
the national security implications of this.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:17.530
There <i>have</i> to be issues on that front.

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:20.680
Overseeing their citizens is 
the reason governments exist.

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:24.300
And you're saying PeopleLog 
is going to muscle in on that?

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:25.790
I believe so.

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:31.470
In the next ten years, it will likely supplant 
over half of existing government services.

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:34.610
It wouldn't be wrong to call this 
an innovation-led revolution.

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:37.360
One that could demolish the 
very concept of government.

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:50.010
You scared me!

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:52.680
I never thought I'd run 
into a samurai out here.

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:57.290
Are you lost?

00:18:57.290 --> 00:18:58.550
Bingo!

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:02.130
I thought I was taking a shortcut, 
but ended up going way off-track...

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:05.620
Well, I've got signal still, 
so it shouldn't be a problem.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.690
Oh, can I upload that picture online?

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:10.800
Young girl, I did not agree to...

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:15.730
M-Murderer!

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.930
<b>Ishikawa Goemon</b>

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.930
<b>Age:
Date of birth:
Nationality:
Job:</b>

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.930
<b>Unknown
Unknown
Unknown
Unknown</b>

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:28.220
<b>Character Evaluation</b>

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:28.220
<b>From the information available from multiple sources, 
there's a high likelihood that he is a descendant of the 
original Ishikawa Goemon, the master thief who lived 
400 years ago.</b>

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:30.350
<b>Even though they have been at odds in the past, 
he is currently believed to be Lupin III's underling.</b>

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:30.350
<b>East Bielefeld University sociologist, Rudolf Streiser, 
has postulated that he Middle Eastern urban legend 
of "the samurai who sliced a train" is, in fact, 
based on Ishikawa Goemon.</b>

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:32.730
<b>Lupin III's underling</b>

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:35.060
"Underling"?!

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:48.860
Jigen...

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.870
Hey. Did you use PeopleLog
 to find me, too, Pops?

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:08.390
I can track you down perfectly 
well without that nonsense.

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:13.470
Old acquaintances are 
having a conversation.

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:15.410
Keep out of it, would you?

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:17.010
From the look on your face...

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:19.640
you've got something to tell me.

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.160
Fujiko's been caught.

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:26.130
PeopleLog made her easy to track down.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:28.900
In the end, she gave herself up.

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:39.680
Times have changed.

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:42.870
You're all relics now.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:46.930
The very concept of thieves... 
<i>Especially</i> Lupin III...

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:49.040
You're old news!

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:18.980
We prepare to infiltrate
 the enemy's fortress.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.810
However, when we do so, all of our 
actions are predicted by PeopleLog.

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:26.280
To be able to push us to our limits... 
This man who goes by the name of Enzo...

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:27.600
Is he a man? Or a monster?

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.940
Lupin, I have no interest in 
a machine's assessment of me.

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:35.070
I do not want an answer divined 
from data. I want one from you.

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:37.920
In your eyes, what am I?

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:40.310
Next time: Answer me, Zantetsuken.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:44.910
<b>Answer me, Zantetsuken</b>

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:43.510
Yes. My blade has always held the answer.
